/**
\defgroup distrib_start Starting values
\brief Sample summaries and starting parameters for univariate distributions on the host.

This module computes a summary of an observation sample and derives starting parameter values for a univariate distribution (`distrib`): the closed-form maximum likelihood estimate (MLE) where one exists, or a moment or robust estimate otherwise. Include `<laplace/distrib/generic_start.hpp>`, which includes `<laplace/distrib/core.hpp>`; every type and function lives in namespace `laplace::distrib` and is called as `laplace::distrib::NAME`. Every function in this module runs on the host only, and none is marked `LAPLACE_HD`. Given a `Kokkos::View` in any memory space, `summarize_sample()` copies it to the host once. The starting values are formulas in the summary statistics, so the page has no accuracy table; the summary takes time linear in the sample size \f$n\f$.

## Sample summary

For a sample \f$y_1, \dots, y_n\f$ of size \f$n\f$, \ref laplace::distrib::summarize_sample(std::vector<double>) "summarize_sample()" populates a \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_sample_summary "distrib_sample_summary" struct. The full-sample fields hold `count` (\f$n\f$), `minimum` (\f$\min_i y_i\f$), `maximum` (\f$\max_i y_i\f$), `mean` (\f$\bar{y} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n y_i\f$), and `variance` with divisor \f$n\f$ (\f$s^2 = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n (y_i - \bar{y})^2\f$).

The order-statistic fields `lower_quartile` (\f$q_{0.25}\f$), `median` (\f$q_{0.5}\f$), and `upper_quartile` (\f$q_{0.75}\f$) are the sample quantiles of type 7 (Hyndman and Fan, 1996). For ordered observations \f$y_{(1)} \le \dots \le y_{(n)}\f$ and probability \f$p \in (0, 1)\f$, let \f$h = (n - 1) p\f$ and \f$j = \lfloor h \rfloor\f$; the type-7 quantile is
\f[
q_p = \begin{cases} y_{(j+1)} + (h - j)\bigl(y_{(j+2)} - y_{(j+1)}\bigr), & j + 1 < n, \\ y_{(n)}, & j + 1 = n. \end{cases}
\f]
The quartiles are computed by selection (partial sorting) in time linear in \f$n\f$. From the median, `mean_abs_dev_median` is \f$\bar{d} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n |y_i - q_{0.5}|\f$.

Four additional fields summarize the positive observations (\f$y_i > 0\f$) only: `positive_count` is their number \f$n_+\f$, `mean_log` is \f$\bar{z}_+ = \frac{1}{n_+}\sum_{y_i > 0} \log y_i\f$, `variance_log` is \f$s_z^2 = \frac{1}{n_+}\sum_{y_i > 0} (\log y_i - \bar{z}_+)^2\f$ with divisor \f$n_+\f$, and `mean_inverse` is \f$\bar{r}_+ = \frac{1}{n_+}\sum_{y_i > 0} 1/y_i\f$. An empty sample (\f$n = 0\f$) sets `count` and all other fields to \f$0\f$. When \f$n_+ = 0\f$, the four positive-only fields are all \f$0\f$.

## Starting values and open domains

Given a distribution `prototype` and a summary, \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_start_value "distrib_start_value()" returns `prototype.start_value(summary)`, a copy of `prototype` whose natural parameters are set from `summary`. The `prototype` carries any fields that are not natural parameters, such as the `size` field of `binomial_distrib`; for all other distributions, any value of the type serves, such as `gaussian1_distrib{}`. A distribution without a `start_value` member is rejected at compile time by a `static_assert` with the message `"distrib_start_value: this distrib has no start_value member; supply a start"`, so the caller supplies its own starting parameters in that case.

Every `start_value` member returns parameters strictly inside their open natural domains \f$(l, u)\f$ (declared in `params[j]` on \ref distrib_core "the core distribution page") by passing each raw value \f$x\f$ through \ref laplace::distrib::start_inside "start_inside()". When \f$x \le l\f$ at a finite lower bound \f$l\f$, the result is \f$10^{-8}\max(|x|, 1)\f$ if \f$l = 0\f$, and \f$l + 16\varepsilon|l|\f$ otherwise, where \f$\varepsilon\f$ is double-precision machine epsilon. When \f$x \ge u\f$, the result is \f$10^{300}\f$ if \f$u = +\infty\f$, \f$-10^{-8}\f$ if \f$u = 0\f$, and \f$u - 16\varepsilon|u|\f$ for any other finite upper bound. Inputs strictly inside \f$(l, u)\f$ and `NaN` inputs are returned unchanged. For example, on a sample of zeros under `bernoulli_distrib`, the maximum likelihood estimate \f$\mu = 0\f$ lies outside the open domain \f$(0, 1)\f$, so `start_value()` returns \f$\mu = 10^{-8}\f$.

## The starting value taken from the sample

In addition to the summary overload above, \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_start_value(const D&, const std::vector<double>&) "distrib_start_value(prototype, y)" accepts a `std::vector<double>` sample directly. When the distribution declares `start_value(y)`, an `if constexpr (requires { prototype.start_value(y); })` check calls that member without building a summary; otherwise it calls \ref laplace::distrib::summarize_sample(std::vector<double>) "summarize_sample(y)" once and returns `prototype.start_value(summary)`, so a distribution with only `start_value(summary)` needs no sample member. The view overload \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_start_value(const D&, const Kokkos::View<const double*, Props...>&) "distrib_start_value(prototype, view)" takes a `Kokkos::View<const double*, Props...>` in any memory space, copies it to the host once with `Kokkos::create_mirror_view_and_copy`, and delegates to the vector form, mirroring \ref laplace::distrib::summarize_sample(const Kokkos::View<const double*, Props...>&) "summarize_sample(view)". Because \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_sample_summary "distrib_sample_summary" holds only the statistics that several distributions share, a distribution whose starting value rests on a statistic used by no other `distrib` declares `start_value(y)` and computes that statistic itself, so the summary does not grow one field per distribution and no call pays for a statistic its distribution never reads.

On a sample of \f$n = 10^6\f$ observations with one thread using gcc 14.2 at `-O2`, the best of ten repetitions of the full summary takes 13.7 ms, of which 13.1 ms are the three selections for the median and quartiles while the mean and variance alone take 1.1 ms. A pair of circular means, the mean of \f$\cos y_i\f$ and the mean of \f$\sin y_i\f$, takes 14.9 ms on its own because each observation costs a sine and a cosine, more than the whole summary, which is why the summary stays as it is. A distribution that declares both members is served by the sample one, and a distribution that declares neither is rejected at compile time.

The following call passes a `std::vector<double>` to `gaussian1_distrib`, which declares `start_value(summary)` only; the same call on a distribution that declares `start_value(y)` goes straight to that member:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/distrib/gaussian1.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/generic_start.hpp>
#include <vector>

double compute_gaussian_start() {
  const std::vector<double> y = {1.2, 2.5, 1.8, 3.1, 2.4};
  const auto g = laplace::distrib::distrib_start_value(laplace::distrib::gaussian1_distrib{}, y);
  return g.mu;
}
\endcode

## Starting values by distribution

The table below lists the nineteen distributions with a `start_value` member, using the summary quantities above together with \f$s = \sqrt{s^2}\f$, the `size` field \f$N\f$ of `binomial_distrib`, and Euler's constant \f$\gamma \approx 0.5772\f$.

| Distribution | Parameters | Formula | Exact MLE |
|---|---|---|---|
| \ref distrib_bernoulli "bernoulli_distrib" | \f$\mu\f$ | \f$\mu = \bar{y}\f$ | Yes |
| \ref distrib_binomial "binomial_distrib" | \f$\mu\f$ | \f$\mu = \bar{y} / N\f$ | Yes |
| \ref distrib_cauchy "cauchy_distrib" | \f$(\mu, \sigma)\f$ | \f$\mu = q_{0.5}\f$, \f$\sigma = (q_{0.75} - q_{0.25})/2\f$ | No (robust) |
| \ref distrib_chisq "chisq_distrib" | \f$\mu\f$ | \f$\mu = \bar{y}\f$ | No (moment) |
| \ref distrib_exponential "exponential_distrib" | \f$\mu\f$ | \f$\mu = \bar{y}\f$ | Yes |
| \ref distrib_gamma1 "gamma1_distrib" | \f$(\mu, \phi)\f$ | \f$\mu = \bar{y}\f$, \f$\phi = s^2 / \bar{y}^2\f$ | \f$\mu\f$ only (\f$\phi\f$ moment) |
| \ref distrib_gamma2 "gamma2_distrib" | \f$(\mu, \sigma^2)\f$ | \f$\mu = \bar{y}\f$, \f$\sigma^2 = s^2\f$ | \f$\mu\f$ only (\f$\sigma^2\f$ moment) |
| \ref distrib_gaussian1 "gaussian1_distrib" | \f$(\mu, \sigma)\f$ | \f$\mu = \bar{y}\f$, \f$\sigma = s\f$ | Yes |
| \ref distrib_gaussian2 "gaussian2_distrib" | \f$(\mu, v)\f$ | \f$\mu = \bar{y}\f$, \f$v = s^2\f$ | Yes |
| \ref distrib_gaussian3 "gaussian3_distrib" | \f$(\mu, \tau)\f$ | \f$\mu = \bar{y}\f$, \f$\tau = 1 / s^2\f$ | Yes |
| \ref distrib_geometric "geometric_distrib" | \f$\mu\f$ | \f$\mu = \bar{y}\f$ | Yes |
| \ref distrib_gumbel "gumbel_distrib" | \f$(\mu, \sigma)\f$ | \f$\mu = \bar{y} - \gamma \sqrt{6 s^2}/\pi\f$, \f$\sigma = \sqrt{6 s^2}/\pi\f$ | No (moment) |
| \ref distrib_invgauss "invgauss_distrib" | \f$(\mu, \phi)\f$ | \f$\mu = \bar{y}\f$, \f$\phi = \bar{r}_+ - 1/\bar{y}\f$ | Yes |
| \ref distrib_laplace1 "laplace1_distrib" | \f$(\mu, \sigma)\f$ | \f$\mu = q_{0.5}\f$, \f$\sigma = \bar{d}\f$ | Yes |
| \ref distrib_laplace2 "laplace2_distrib" | \f$(\mu, \lambda)\f$ | \f$\mu = q_{0.5}\f$, \f$\lambda = 1 / \bar{d}\f$ | Yes |
| \ref distrib_logistic "logistic_distrib" | \f$(\mu, \sigma)\f$ | \f$\mu = \bar{y}\f$, \f$\sigma = \sqrt{3 s^2}/\pi\f$ | No (moment) |
| \ref distrib_lognormal1 "lognormal1_distrib" | \f$(\mu, v)\f$ | \f$\mu = \bar{z}_+\f$, \f$v = s_z^2\f$ | Yes |
| \ref distrib_lognormal2 "lognormal2_distrib" | \f$(m, v)\f$ | \f$m = \bar{y}\f$, \f$v = s^2\f$ | No (moment) |
| \ref distrib_poisson "poisson_distrib" | \f$\mu\f$ | \f$\mu = \bar{y}\f$ | Yes |

Because the Cauchy law has no moments, `cauchy_distrib` uses the median and half the interquartile range as a robust estimate. For `laplace1_distrib` and `laplace2_distrib` at even \f$n\f$, every value of \f$\mu\f$ between the two middle order statistics maximizes the likelihood, and the type-7 median (their midpoint) is one of them.

## Example

The following host function summarizes a sample of positive values for `gamma2_distrib` and a sample of counts for `binomial_distrib` with `size` equal to 10:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/distrib/binomial.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/gamma2.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/generic_start.hpp>
#include <vector>

double compute_starts() {
  const std::vector<double> y = {1.2, 2.5, 1.8, 3.1, 2.4};
  const auto s = laplace::distrib::summarize_sample(y);
  const auto g = laplace::distrib::distrib_start_value(laplace::distrib::gamma2_distrib{}, s);

  const std::vector<double> counts = {2.0, 4.0, 3.0, 5.0, 1.0};
  const auto s2 = laplace::distrib::summarize_sample(counts);
  const auto b = laplace::distrib::distrib_start_value(laplace::distrib::binomial_distrib{0.0, 10.0}, s2);
  return g.mu + b.mu;
}
\endcode

## Module functions

- \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_sample_summary "distrib_sample_summary" — summary statistics of a sample
- \ref laplace::distrib::summarize_sample(std::vector<double>) "summarize_sample(y)" — summary of a sample in a `std::vector<double>`
- \ref laplace::distrib::summarize_sample(const Kokkos::View<const double*, Props...>&) "summarize_sample(view)" — summary of a sample in a `Kokkos::View`, copied to the host once
- \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_start_value "distrib_start_value()" — starting parameters of a distribution from a sample summary
- \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_start_value(const D&, const std::vector<double>&) "distrib_start_value(prototype, y)" — starting parameters of a distribution from a sample in a `std::vector<double>`
- \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_start_value(const D&, const Kokkos::View<const double*, Props...>&) "distrib_start_value(prototype, view)" — starting parameters of a distribution from a sample in a `Kokkos::View`, copied to the host once
- \ref laplace::distrib::start_inside "start_inside()" — moves a value strictly inside a parameter's open natural domain

## References

- R. J. Hyndman and Y. Fan (1996), Sample Quantiles in Statistical Packages, The American Statistician, 50(4), 361–365. https://doi.org/10.1080/00031305.1996.10473566
*/
